.TH ASSEMBLY 5
.SH NAME
assembly \- the parts of an assembly, their places and their order
.SH DESCRIPTION
An assembly file, in the language of
.IR plicad (7),
names the parts of an assembly, one a line, each with its place in the
world, and the order of the part lines is the
.IR "assembly sequence" :
the order in which the parts go together.
.IR place (1)
reads it and writes the parts in place, and
.B place \-s
writes a scene for
.IR view (1),
which steps through the sequence.
.PP
.RS
.nf
materials FILE
material NAME R G B ROUGHNESS DENSITY
step DIST DEG
clearance MM
part NAME FILE [STEP]... [material MATERIAL] [TAIL]
image NAME FILE on PART X0 Y0 Z0 X1 Y1 Z1 X2 Y2 Z2
.fi
.RE
.PP
where each
.I STEP
is a
.BI move " DX DY DZ"
or a
.BI turn " x|y|z DEG"
.RB [ about
.IR "X Y Z" ],
and the optional
.I TAIL
is
.B path
.IR STEP ...
.RB [ step
.IR "DIST DEG" ]
or
.B with
.IR NAME .
.PP
A
.B material
gives a material, or a finish, as
.IR plicad (7)
says; a
.B materials
line reads a file of material lines.
.SS Parts
A part line names the part, new in the file, and its stream
.RI ( stream (5)):
of a folded part
.RI ( smfold (1))
or of solids
.RI ( fillet (1)).
A part of a stream of more than one part takes the name NAME/SUB, SUB
its name in the stream.
The steps of its place apply in order, a turn about the world origin
unless it names a point.
.B material
gives it a material of the file: its color, and the density by which
.IR measure (1)
weighs it; the coats of a folded part
.RI ( sm (5))
name materials of the file too.
.SS Subassemblies
The file of a part line may be another assembly file, one whose first
statement is not
.BR PliCad :
a subassembly, read as this page says, relative to its own directory,
and placed as one unit by the steps of the line.
Its parts take the name NAME/SUB, SUB the name of the part there, so
names nest: in the assembly
.PP
.RS
.nf
part front panel.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
whose panel.txt holds
.B part knob knob.txt
and whose knob.txt holds
.BR "part cap cap.st" ,
the cap is front/knob/cap, and every program names it so.
A subassembly keeps its own names and materials; a material of one
name must be the same in all.
Its line takes no
.BR material .
A file that holds itself, through any chain of subassemblies, is
refused with the chain of the files.
.PP
The assembly sequence nests too: a subassembly goes together first,
in its own sequence and in its own frame, and then its line brings it
in as one unit, by its own path or with the part it names.
.SS Insertion paths
.B path
gives the
.I insertion path
of the part, or of a subassembly as one unit: the motion that brings
it from its start pose to its place.
The steps run in order, a turn about the origin of the part at its
place unless it names a point.
The start pose is the place with the steps undone, the last one first;
so
.PP
.RS
.nf
part lid lid.st move 0 0 50 path move 0 0 \-30
.fi
.RE
.PP
starts 30 mm above its place and comes down.
.PP
.B with
.I NAME
makes the part travel with the part NAME of a line above it, fixed to
it: the two go in together along the path of NAME, as a screw goes in
with the bracket that holds it.
A part with neither goes in at its place, with no path.
.PP
.IR place (1)
places every part at its place and takes no notice of paths and of
.BR with ;
.B place \-s
writes them into the scene.
.PP
.IR assemble (1)
tries each path in samples:
.BI step " DIST DEG"
gives the most a sample of a path of the file moves its parts, in mm,
and turns them, in degrees; the same words after a path set those of
that path.
A file of paths needs a step.
.BI clearance " MM"
makes assemble say each pair of parts that passes nearer than that on a
path of the file.
.SS Pictures
An
.B image
lays a picture (PPM or PAM, see
.IR scene (5))
on the part PART of a line above, its corners X0 (lower left), X1
(lower right) and X2 (upper left) in the frame of the part, where its
stream has it: the picture moves with the part.
Only
.B place \-s
writes pictures, into the scene.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
.nf
include params
materials materials
part chassis out/chassis.st material black
part board out/board.st move 10 10 Base material green \e
    path move 0 0 \-20
part nut1 out/nut.st move 20 20 Base with board
part cover out/cover.st move 0 0 H material black \e
    path move 0 \-50 0 turn x 30 about 0 0 H
image label label.pam on cover 10 \-1 5 60 \-1 5 10 \-1 25
.fi
.RE
.PP
The board drops 20 mm into the chassis with its nut; the cover slides
50 mm toward \-y, and then turns 30 degrees about the line along x at
the height H to its place, its label with it.
.SH SEE ALSO
plicad(7), place(1), view(1), scene(5), stream(5), sm(5)
